{"name":"Fainting Light","hex":"#1f2847","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fainting","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of faint"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of collapsing into a state of temporary unconsciousness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fainting"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Syncope (medicine)","blocks":[{"text":"Syncope, commonly known as fainting or passing out, is a loss of consciousness and muscle strength characterized by a fast onset, short duration, and spontaneous recovery. It is caused by a decrease in blood flow to the brain, typically from low blood pressure. There are sometimes symptoms before the loss of consciousness such as lightheadedness, sweating, pale skin, blurred vision, nausea, vomiting, or feeling warm. Syncope may also be associated with a short episode of muscle twitching. Psychiatric causes can also be determined when a patient experiences fear, anxiety, or panic; particularly before a stressful event, usually medical in nature. When consciousness and muscle strength are not completely lost, it is called presyncope. It is recommended that presyncope be treated the same as syncope."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Pietro_Longhi_027.jpg/330px-Pietro_Longhi_027.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syncope_(medicine)"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blausen_0100_Bradycardia_Fainting.png/500px-Blausen_0100_Bradycardia_Fainting.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Blausen_0100_Bradycardia_Fainting.png/500px-Blausen_0100_Bradycardia_Fainting.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Syncope from bradycardia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0100_Bradycardia_Fainting.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg/960px-DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg/1280px-DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg/960px-DVA2555_%28CardioNetworks_ECGpedia%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADVA2555_(CardioNetworks_ECGpedia).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimed